  "result": "Think of a restaurant with a waiter, a kitchen and a customer.\n\nYou're the customer, and the model plays you. You can't walk into the kitchen. You can only read the menu and write an order on a slip: \"one pad thai, no peanuts.\" That slip is the tool call. It's only a request, and nothing has been cooked yet.\n\nThe waiter, who is the program, takes the slip to the kitchen. The kitchen does the real work. The waiter brings the plate back, and you read it and decide what to do next: order dessert, or say \"that's all, thanks.\"\n\nThe menu matters because you can only order what's listed, and the descriptions are how you know what each dish is.\n\n**Why this works:** the model only ever produces text. A tool call is text that says \"please run this, with these details.\" Something outside the model does the actual running.\n\n**Where it breaks down:** a customer has a body and could walk into the kitchen. The model can't.\n\nFor your makerspace assistant, \"Is the laser cutter free Friday?\" is the customer's request. In that picture, what's the slip, and what's the kitchen?",
